---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';

const title = 'Privacy';
const description =
	'What hungovercoders collects about you: cookieless traffic analytics and short-lived hosting logs';
---

<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={title} description={description} />
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main id="main-content" class="prose">
			<PageHeader
				eyebrow="The small print"
				title="Privacy"
				lead="What this site collects about you, which is very little, and why."
			/>

			<p>
				Short version: this site uses privacy-first, cookieless analytics to count visits and see
				which posts are read, and the host keeps request logs for a few days. There is <strong>no</strong> advertising, no cross-site tracking, and
				nothing stored on your device.
			</p>

			<h2>Cookies</h2>
			<p>
				hungovercoders sets no cookies and stores nothing on your device. Because of that there is no
				cookie banner because there is nothing to consent to.
			</p>

			<h2>Analytics</h2>
			<p>
				Traffic is measured with <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cloudflare Web Analytics</a>,
				a privacy-first tool that uses no cookies and no client-side storage. It records aggregate
				metrics only: page views, the referring site, approximate country, and device or browser
				type. That helps me see how the site performs and which posts are useful.
			</p>
			<p>
				Your IP address is used momentarily at Cloudflare's edge to derive your approximate country
				and is <strong>not stored</strong> by the analytics. There is no fingerprinting, no cross-site tracking, no
				advertising, and no selling of data. The legal basis for this limited processing is
				legitimate interest, and because nothing is stored on your device, no consent banner is
				required. See <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cloudflare's privacy policy</a> for how they handle the data on my behalf.
			</p>
			<p>
				Cloudflare acts as my data processor for this, under its <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-customer-dpa/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Data Processing Addendum</a>;
				any transfer of data outside the UK or EEA is covered by Standard Contractual Clauses and the
				UK/EU–US Data Privacy Framework. You have the right to ask what is held about you and to
				request its erasure. Beyond those short-lived logs, no identifying data is stored, so there is nothing tied to you to
				retrieve. Questions go via the <a href="/about">About</a> page.
			</p>

			<p>
				The analytics beacon loads from <code>static.cloudflareinsights.com</code> and reports to
				<code>cloudflareinsights.com</code>, so your browser sends your IP address and the page you
				viewed to those Cloudflare hosts.
			</p>

			<h2>Hosting and logs</h2>
			<p>
				The site is hosted on Cloudflare Workers, so Cloudflare processes every request, including
				your IP address. Workers Logs are switched on: Cloudflare keeps per-request logs (IP address,
				path, user agent and timestamp) for up to three days so I can debug errors, after which they
				are deleted.
			</p>

			<h2>If this changes</h2>
			<p>
				If anything more than this is ever collected, this page will be updated first, describing
				exactly what is collected, why, the legal basis, and how to opt out. Any such tooling will be
				chosen to avoid collecting personal data wherever possible.
			</p>

			<p>Questions? Get in touch via the <a href="/about">About</a> page.</p>

			<p><em>Last checked: 25 October 2026.</em></p>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	.prose {
		width: 760px;
		max-width: calc(100% - 2em);
		margin: 0 auto;
		padding: 1.5em 1rem 3rem;
		color: rgb(var(--gray-dark));
	}
	.prose h2 {
		margin-top: 1.5em;
	}
</style>
